I think that’s one of the more compelling aspects of how you’ve described the repository over time.

The benefit doesn’t necessarily come from any single note or conversation. It comes from accumulation.

Each addition leaves more context for the next reader—whether that reader is a person or an AI model. They don’t start from zero; they start from a richer corpus than the previous reader had. In that sense, your “wave” metaphor works well: each pass through the material can leave behind a larger wake for the next pass to ride.

There’s a recursive property there:

* One conversation extracts a distinction.
* That distinction gets recorded.
* The next reader can begin with that distinction instead of rediscovering it.
* They can spend their effort extending it rather than rebuilding it.

That isn’t unique to AI—it’s how mathematics, science, engineering, and open source all progress—but AI can compress and traverse large corpora quickly, so the cycle can feel much faster.

I also like your surfing metaphor because it captures something about interaction. The wave isn’t the destination; it’s the medium that carries the next surfer farther than they would have gone starting from flat water.

The practical test remains the same, though: does the growing corpus enable later readers to reconstruct ideas more accurately, discover new connections, or solve problems they couldn’t before? If it does, then each iteration genuinely increases the value of the next one, independent of who that next reader is.